rty=3D"name">Abstract</h3><div role=3D"paragraph">Scheduling timetables for=
 courses at universities is known as the University Course Timetabling Prob=
lem (UCTTP), an NP-hard problem that becomes harder as the data size grows.=
 Although many approaches have been investigated, exact optimisation is oft=
en prohibitive at scale, and standalone metaheuristics are parameter-sensit=
ive, stagnating or converging slowly. In this paper, a hybrid Genetic Algor=
ithm=E2=80=93Simulated Annealing (GA=E2=80=93SA) method is proposed in whic=
h GA provides global exploration, and SA is applied conditionally as local =
refinement. Considering feasibility as the primary objective, candidate tim=
etables are evaluated by counting hard-constraint violations, including roo=
m-time clashes, instructor conflicts, unavailability, and capacity breaches=
, and the algorithm terminates once a zero-violation solution is found. The=
 proposed hybrid approach is evaluated on the ITC-2007 Curriculum-Based Cou=
rse Timetabling benchmark and scaled instances derived from it. Results sho=
w consistent feasibility and better scalability on enlarged instances than =
GA-only and SA-only baselines.</div></section><section id=3D"plain-language=
