extent=3D"bodymatter" property=3D"articleBody" typeof=3D"Text"><div class=
=3D"core-container"><section id=3D"sec-1"><h2>1 Introduction</h2><div role=
=3D"paragraph">University Course Timetabling Problem (UCTTP) is a well-know=
n NP-hard optimisation problem in which courses must be assigned to timeslo=
ts and rooms while satisfying numerous institutional constraints [<a href=
=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" role=3D"do=
c-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-1">5</a>]. Althoug=
h exact methods can solve small instances, they become impractical for medi=
um to large institutions as problem size and constraint density increase, l=
eading to rapid growth in solution time and search complexity. Currently ap=
proximately 254 million students are enrolled in universities all over the =
world, and this number has more than doubled in the past twenty years and i=
s expected to grow &nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/37=
93811.3793816#Bib0014" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0014" id=
=3D"core-Bib0014-1">14</a>]. In practice, large universities manage complex=
 teaching operations involving tens of thousands of students and staff, mul=
tiple campuses, diverse delivery modes, and frequent changes during the pla=
nning horizon. For example, Curtin University, which is located in Western =
Australia, manages over 65,000 students, 6,000 staff and more than 10,000 c=
ourse completions as of 2024. As a result, producing a conflict-free timeta=
ble remains difficult, and manual construction is generally infeasible at s=
cale.</div><div role=3D"paragraph">Many approaches have been studied for UC=
TTP. Exact methods (e.g., integer and constraint programming) can produce o=
ptimal or bounded solutions but often scale poorly as instances grow [<a hr=
ef=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" role=3D"=
doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-2">5</a>]. As a =
result, practice favours heuristics and metaheuristics such as GA, SA, tabu=
 search, and other local-search methods for their scalability and flexibili=
ty [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005"=
 role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-3">5</a=
>, <a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0008" =
role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0008" id=3D"core-Bib0008-1">8</a>=
]. Hybrid approaches that combine global search with local improvement have=
 also been proposed to speed convergence and improve feasibility on highly =
constrained cases [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3=
793816#Bib0005" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-=
Bib0005-4">5</a>]. Despite this progress, efficiently reaching feasibility =
for large-scale instances remains challenging, motivating further work on h=
ybrid designs and scalable benchmarking.</div><div role=3D"paragraph">Timet=
abling systems are therefore critical decision-support tools for allocating=
 time and room resources under multiple constraints while supporting educat=
ional objectives and operational requirements [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.or=
g/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0012" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml=
-rid=3D"Bib0012" id=3D"core-Bib0012-1">12</a>]. The problem=E2=80=99s compl=
exity arises from the need to prevent overlaps for students and staff, resp=
ect room availability and capacity, and accommodate unavailability and othe=
r institutional policies. Constraints are typically categorised as <i>hard<=
/i> (must always be satisfied to ensure feasibility) and <i>soft</i> (prefe=
rences that improve timetable quality) [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/f=
ull/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0012" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D=
"Bib0012" id=3D"core-Bib0012-2">12</a>]. Consistent with prior surveys [<a =
href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" role=
=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-5">5</a>], o=
ur objective is to generate feasible timetables by satisfying all hard cons=
traints, including conflict avoidance, adherence to room constraints, and r=
espect for timeslot unavailability. Soft-constraint optimisation, while imp=
ortant for improving solution quality, is treated as secondary and left for=
 future work. This work focuses on feasibility by enforcing the hard constr=
aints defined in the ITC-2007 [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.11=
45/3793811.3793816#Bib0006" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0006"=
 id=3D"core-Bib0006-1">6</a>] specifications, which are listed as follows:<=
/div><div data-type=3D"bullet" role=3D"list"><div id=3D"list1" role=3D"list=
item"><div class=3D"label">=E2=80=A2</div><div class=3D"content"><div role=
=3D"paragraph"><b>Lectures:</b> All courses must be assigned to distinct pe=
riods.</div></div></div><div id=3D"list2" role=3D"listitem"><div class=3D"l=
abel">=E2=80=A2</div><div class=3D"content"><div role=3D"paragraph"><b>Room=
 Occupancy:</b> Two lectures cannot take place in the same room in the same=
 period.</div></div></div><div id=3D"list3" role=3D"listitem"><div class=3D=
"label">=E2=80=A2</div><div class=3D"content"><div role=3D"paragraph"><b>Co=
nflicts:</b> Taught by the same teacher, all must be scheduled in different=
 periods.</div></div></div><div id=3D"list4" role=3D"listitem"><div class=
=3D"label">=E2=80=A2</div><div class=3D"content"><div role=3D"paragraph"><b=
>Unavailable Period:</b> Certain courses are unavailable during specific pe=
riods, and no classes can be scheduled in those timeslots.</div></div></div=
></div><div role=3D"paragraph">The overarching aim of this research is to d=
evelop a novel hybrid optimisation approach that combines a Genetic algorit=
hm (GA) with Simulated annealing (SA) to address the University Course Time=
tabling Problem (UCTTP). The GA provides global exploration by evolving a p=
opulation of candidate timetables through selection, crossover, and mutatio=
n, thereby maintaining diversity and enabling broad search across the solut=
ion space. SA is integrated as an intensification mechanism: after GA gener=
ates new offspring (or updates an elite subset each generation), an SA-base=
d local search is applied to each selected timetable to reduce hard-constra=
int violations by exploring a neighbourhood of small schedule modifications=
 (e.g., lecture moves or swaps). Unlike purely greedy local search, SA acce=
pts occasional non-improving moves according to a temperature-controlled pr=
obability, which helps escape local minima and improves the likelihood of r=
eaching feasibility in highly constrained instances. The locally improved i=
ndividuals are then returned to the GA population for replacement and furth=
er evolution, yielding a balance between exploration (GA) and exploitation =
(SA) aimed primarily at efficiently attaining feasible timetables under the=
 ITC-2007 hard constraints. The method is evaluated using the ITC-2007 benc=
hmark instances and newly constructed scalable variants derived from them w=
hich are further compared with standalone GA and standalone SA baselines.</=
div><div role=3D"paragraph">The main contributions of this paper are summar=
ised as follows:</div><div data-type=3D"bullet" role=3D"list"><div id=3D"li=
st5" role=3D"listitem"><div class=3D"label">=E2=80=A2</div><div class=3D"co=
ntent"><div role=3D"paragraph">Implement a GA+SA hybrid solution for UCTTP =
with open-source code and related test results, providing a baseline for fu=
ture research.</div></div></div><div id=3D"list6" role=3D"listitem"><div cl=
ass=3D"label">=E2=80=A2</div><div class=3D"content"><div role=3D"paragraph"=
>Expand larger-scale datasets (2=E2=80=9310 =C3=97 ITC2007) and validate th=
em, providing valuable results for comparative studies.</div></div></div><d=
iv id=3D"list7" role=3D"listitem"><div class=3D"label">=E2=80=A2</div><div =
class=3D"content"><div role=3D"paragraph">Demonstrate that the hybrid metho=
d can achieve feasible solutions efficiently, especially for scalable insta=
nces compared to standalone GA and SA.</div></div></div></div><div role=3D"=
paragraph">The rest of this paper are organised as follows: Section &nbsp;<=
a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#sec-2">2</a> =
reviews literatures and existing approaches. Section &nbsp;<a href=3D"https=
://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#sec-4">4</a> presents the pr=
oblem and outlines the methodology, including the benchmark, algorithm desi=
gn, and experimental setup. Section &nbsp;<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi=
/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#sec-5">5</a> compares hybrid results with sin=
gle-solution methods (e.g., GA or SA alone). Section &nbsp;<a href=3D"https=
://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#sec-6">6</a> presents the co=
nclusion with future directions.</div></section><section id=3D"sec-2"><h2>2=
 Literature Review</h2><div role=3D"paragraph">Many studies have explored d=
ifferent algorithms to solve the UTCCP problem. Common algorithms include h=
euristic algorithms, integer algorithms, and meta-heuristic algorithms. The=
se algorithms are the foundation for more advanced scheduling solutions.</d=
iv><div role=3D"paragraph">Existing school timetabling algorithms can be di=
vided into six main categories &nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/ful=
l/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"B=
ib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-6">5</a>]: mathematical algorithms (including in=
teger programming, constraint programming, etc.), meta-heuristic algorithms=
, which include Genetic Algorithms (GA) and single solution-based methods s=
uch as Simulated Annealing (SA), graph colouring algorithms, hybrid approac=
hes, matheuristic approaches, and hyperheuristic approaches. Among these al=
gorithms, mathematical optimisation techniques can find good or optimal sol=
utions under both hard and soft constraints for small-scale problems, but t=
he scalability is limited. Metaheuristic algorithms, while able to quickly =
find high-quality solutions, cannot guarantee optimality &nbsp;[<a href=3D"=
https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0017" role=3D"doc-bi=
blioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0017" id=3D"core-Bib0017-1">17</a>]. Exact meth=
ods can obtain optimal solutions but are less efficient when dealing with m=
edium-sized and large problems &nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/ful=
l/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0012" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"B=
ib0012" id=3D"core-Bib0012-3">12</a>]. Therefore, different algorithms have=
 their advantages and disadvantages, mainly depending on how to optimise an=
d in which scenarios to apply.</div><div role=3D"paragraph">Saviniec et al.=
&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib00=
16" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0016" id=3D"core-Bib0016-1">1=
6</a>] categorise UCTTP methods into: (i) OR-based techniques (e.g., graph-=
colouring heuristics, integer programming, and constraint logic programming=
) that use mathematical models to satisfy hard constraints and improve soft=
-constraint satisfaction; (ii) single-solution metaheuristics (e.g., tabu s=
earch, neighbourhood search, and SA) that iteratively refine one timetable;=
 (iii) population-based methods (e.g., GA, ant colony optimisation, and par=
ticle swarm optimisation) that search using multiple candidates; and (iv) h=
yperheuristic and hybrid approaches that combine heuristics to improve flex=
ibility and performance. They also report successful applications, includin=
g an Integer Programming (IP) model at a Sri Lankan university that reduced=
 modelling complexity while improving staff and student preferences [<a hre=
f=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0013" role=3D"d=
oc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0013" id=3D"core-Bib0013-1">13</a>], and a=
n SA approach at Izmir Institute of Technology where search/exchange-based =
moves maintained feasibility and reduced soft-constraint violations [<a hre=
f=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0003" role=3D"d=
oc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0003" id=3D"core-Bib0003-1">3</a>].</div><=
div role=3D"paragraph">Furthermore, for the benchmark UCTTP, the single-sol=
ution genetic algorithm is the most commonly used method&nbsp;[<a href=3D"h=
ttps://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0016" role=3D"doc-bib=
lioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0016" id=3D"core-Bib0016-2">16</a>]. However, in=
 the actual UCTTP, the Tabu search algorithm is the most popular. Therefore=
, from the observation, the advanced methods in the benchmark UCTTP are not=
 fully applied in the actual UCTTP. Researchers can apply the advanced meth=
ods in the benchmark UCTTP to the actual UCTTP in academic institutions&nbs=
p;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" =
role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-7">5</a>=
].</div><div role=3D"paragraph">Gu et al.&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.o=
rg/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0009" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xm=
l-rid=3D"Bib0009" id=3D"core-Bib0009-1">9</a>] review integer programming (=
IP) for university timetabling and discuss integrating machine learning (ML=
) with IP. Across 95 IP papers (1990=E2=80=932023), 80 used commercial solv=
ers, most commonly CPLEX. They report commercial solvers are efficient and =
achieve small deviations (typically <span role=3D"math"><span class=3D"core=
-tex"><mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" ta=
bindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"0" style=3D"position: relative;"><mjx-math=
 class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><mjx-mo class=3D"mjx-n"><mjx-c clas=
s=3D"mjx-c3C"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=3D"mjx-n" space=3D"4"><mjx-c c=
lass=3D"mjx-c31"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-n"><mjx-c class=3D"m=
jx-c25"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" =
display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&=
lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">%</mi></math></mjx-assistive-m=
ml></mjx-container></span></span>), including on large instances. For in-ho=
use implementations, Python, Matlab, and Java are the most frequent languag=
es. The review also highlights growing use of ML in timetabling (notably sc=
hool scheduling) and its potential to improve optimisation workflows.</div>=
<div role=3D"paragraph">From these previous studies, it is found that multi=
ple heuristic and metaheuristic algorithms, such as GA and simulated anneal=
ing, have been widely used to solve the UCTTP problem, and mathematical opt=
imisation techniques are also used to solve some small-scale problems. Due =
to the development of artificial intelligence technology, combining these t=
echnologies with machine learning algorithms to apply to university schedul=
ing is also a new idea for future research. Furthermore, Premananda et al.&=
nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib001=
4" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0014" id=3D"core-Bib0014-2">14=
</a>] found that the limitation of metaheuristic algorithms led to academic=
s exploring some hybrid approaches. Combining multiple metaheuristic method=
s can improve scheduling efficiency, such as the integration of genetic alg=
orithms with simulated annealing, aiming to combine the strengths of both m=
ethods to optimise the UCTTP problem.</div><div role=3D"paragraph">Both Gen=
etic Algorithms (GA) and Simulated Annealing (SA) are widely used metaheuri=
stics for UCTTP. GA is well suited to large search spaces due to its popula=
tion-based global exploration; Ghaemi et al.&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.ac=
m.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0008" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data=
-xml-rid=3D"Bib0008" id=3D"core-Bib0008-2">8</a>] demonstrated its adaptabi=
lity via a modified GA, and Gu et al.&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/d=
oi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0009" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-ri=
d=3D"Bib0009" id=3D"core-Bib0009-2">9</a>] noted its flexibility across dat=
asets through encoding and robust global search. SA, by contrast, is a sing=
le-solution method that performs strong local improvement and can escape lo=
cal optima via probabilistic acceptance; its effectiveness for producing hi=
gh-quality timetables has been reported by Aycan and Ayav&nbsp;[<a href=3D"=
https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0003" role=3D"doc-bi=
blioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0003" id=3D"core-Bib0003-2">3</a>], and its val=
ue in tightly constrained settings is emphasised in surveys [<a href=3D"htt=
ps://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" role=3D"doc-bibli=
oref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-8">5</a>, <a href=3D"http=
s://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0017" role=3D"doc-biblio=
ref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0017" id=3D"core-Bib0017-2">17</a>].</div><div role=
=3D"paragraph">Despite their complementary strengths, GA and SA are often s=
tudied separately, and GA+SA hybrids remain comparatively under-explored. A=
kkan and G=C3=BClc=C3=BC&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.11=
