><h2>3 Assumptions and Problem Formulation</h2><div role=3D"paragraph">The =
University Course Timetabling Problem (UCTTP) is NP-hard due to the combina=
torial growth of feasible assignments as the numbers of courses, rooms, and=
 timeslots increase. Following the ITC-2007 specification and the scalable =
instances derived from it, this paper adopts a feasibility-oriented formula=
tion in which only hard constraints are enforced.</div><section id=3D"sec-3=
-1"><h3>3.1 Assumptions</h3><div role=3D"paragraph">We assume that each lec=
ture event must be assigned to exactly one admissible timeslot and room. Ro=
om=E2=80=93time resources are exclusive (i.e., a room cannot host more than=
 one event in the same timeslot). Dataset-defined restrictions, including l=
ecturer/event unavailability and room capacity limits, must be respected. I=
n this phase of the research, the objective is restricted to satisfying har=
d constraints; soft constraints are deferred to future work.</div></section=
><section id=3D"sec-3-2"><h3>3.2 Problem Formulation</h3><div role=3D"parag=
