n&nbsp;(<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#eq3"=
>3</a>) ensures each course is scheduled once. Equation&nbsp;(<a href=3D"ht=
tps://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#eq4">4</a>) prevents two =
courses from using the same room and timeslot. Equations&nbsp;(<a href=3D"h=
ttps://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#eq5">5</a>) and&nbsp;(<a=
 href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#eq6">6</a>) ha=
ndle hard constraints from the dataset (e.g., unavailable timeslots and cap=
acity limits). Equation&nbsp;(<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.114=
5/3793811.3793816#eq7">7</a>) defines the binary variable domain.</div><div=
 role=3D"paragraph">The objective in&nbsp;(<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/do=
i/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#eq1">1</a>) induces a highly constrained, no=
n-smooth landscape where small changes can trigger multiple violations, so =
greedy local improvement often stagnates. GA provides global exploration th=
rough population diversity and recombination, preserving partial assignment=
s that satisfy subsets of constraints, but may converge slowly when only mi=
nor, targeted repairs remain. SA complements this by local intensification =
via neighbourhood moves and temperature-controlled acceptance&nbsp;(), enab=
ling escape from local minima while reducing violations. A GA+SA hybrid the=
refore combines exploration with refinement, accelerating convergence to <i=
>f</i>(<i>x</i>) =3D 0 and improving feasibility on larger or tighter insta=
nces compared with GA or SA alone.</div></section></section><section id=3D"=
sec-4"><h2>4 Methodology</h2><div role=3D"paragraph">This paper investigate=
s a feasibility-first approach to the University Course Timetabling Problem=
 (UCTTP) by combining a Genetic algorithm (GA) with Simulated annealing (SA=
). Given the strong focus on hard-constraint satisfaction in the ITC-2007 T=
rack 3 benchmark and common practice in the literature [<a href=3D"https://=
dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" role=3D"doc-biblioref"=
 data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-9">5</a>, <a href=3D"https://d=
l.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0015" role=3D"doc-biblioref" =
data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0015" id=3D"core-Bib0015-1">15</a>, <a href=3D"https://d=
l.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0017" role=3D"doc-biblioref" =
data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0017" id=3D"core-Bib0017-6">17</a>], we evaluate three i=
mplementations under a consistent representation and evaluation function: (=
i) a standalone GA, (ii) a standalone SA, and (iii) a hybrid GA+SA approach=
. Following the approaches of&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/=
10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0005" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib=
0005" id=3D"core-Bib0005-10">5</a>, <a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/=
10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0015" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib=
0015" id=3D"core-Bib0015-2">15</a>], the GA design in this study adopts rou=
lette-wheel selection, one-point crossover, and a mutation operator. Chromo=
some representation encodes a timetable solution that contains multiple cou=
rses mapped to (timeslot, room) pairs&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/d=
oi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0008" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-ri=
d=3D"Bib0008" id=3D"core-Bib0008-4">8</a>]. The fitness function is defined=
 as the number of hard constraint violations&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.ac=
m.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0010" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data=
-xml-rid=3D"Bib0010" id=3D"core-Bib0010-1">10</a>, <a href=3D"https://dl.ac=
m.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0018" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data=
-xml-rid=3D"Bib0018" id=3D"core-Bib0018-1">18</a>]. Following&nbsp;[<a href=
=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0003" role=3D"do=
c-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0003" id=3D"core-Bib0003-4">3</a>, <a href=
=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0004" role=3D"do=
c-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0004" id=3D"core-Bib0004-1">4</a>], the SA =
design adopts a cooling schedule and acceptance criteria. The initial tempe=
rature, cooling rate, and minimum threshold values are defined to ensure ex=
ploration in the early stage and convergence as the search progresses. The =
solution acceptance formula follows&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi=
/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0011" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=
=3D"Bib0011" id=3D"core-Bib0011-1">11</a>], allowing worse solutions to be =
accepted with a given probability. The algorithm is developed and tested in=
 three stages to evaluate the contribution. First, a standalone GA is imple=
mented using standard operators to explore the solution result&nbsp;[<a hre=
f=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0015" role=3D"d=
oc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0015" id=3D"core-Bib0015-3">15</a>]. Next,=
 a standalone SA is applied independently to the same problem, allowing a d=
etailed assessment of its local search capabilities. Finally, we use a hybr=
id GA+SA model, which combines the advantages of both GA and SA. This algor=
ithm design enables direct comparison between GA, SA, and the hybrid GA+SA =
