ion><section id=3D"sec-4-2"><h3>4.2 Algorithm Design</h3><section id=3D"sec=
-4-2-1"><h4>4.2.1 Genetic Algorithm (GA).</h4><div role=3D"paragraph"><i>Ch=
romosome representation.</i> Each individual in the population encodes a ti=
metable solution&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/37938=
11.3793816#Bib0015" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0015" id=3D"c=
ore-Bib0015-5">15</a>]. A chromosome lists each gene, mapping a course to a=
 (timeslot, room) pair, and adopts a two-dimensional array structure&nbsp;[=
<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0008" rol=
e=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0008" id=3D"core-Bib0008-5">8</a>]. =
The assignment must respect that only available timeslots can be selected f=
or each course and enable a mapping between courses and their scheduled tim=
eslots and rooms.</div><div role=3D"paragraph"><i>Initial population.</i> T=
he initial population is generated by creating a set of chromosomes randoml=
y. All unavailable timeslot indices are collected at first and only the rem=
aining available slots are considered d&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org=
/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0015" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-=
rid=3D"Bib0015" id=3D"core-Bib0015-6">15</a>]. Each course is assigned a ra=
ndom timeslot and room that do not violate unavailability constraints, a li=
st of valid (timeslot, room) pairs is constructed. If no valid pair is foun=
d, a placeholder (=E2=80=931, None) is used to indicate an unassigned state=
. This approach ensures that the initial population consists of feasible so=
lutions, which can impact the correctness of the final solution and improve=
 the performance in solving timetabling problems&nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://d=
l.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0012" role=3D"doc-biblioref" =
data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0012" id=3D"core-Bib0012-4">12</a>].</div><div role=3D"p=
aragraph"><i>Fitness function.</i> This function is evaluated based on the =
number of hard constraint violations. These hard constraints include room-t=
ime conflicts, where no two courses are allowed to be scheduled in the same=
 room at the same time slot, as well as course unavailability restrictions.=
 A solution that satisfies all hard constraints can result in a fitness val=
ue of 0, representing a perfect timetable. This function is widely accepted=
 in the UCTTP area, according to &nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/f=
ull/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0004" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D=
"Bib0004" id=3D"core-Bib0004-2">4</a>],satisfying all hard constraints is c=
onsidered a primary objective, which is indicated by a zero violation score=
. The fitness function checks each course in the chromosome. If the same ro=
om and time slot are assigned more than once, a penalty point is accumulate=
d plus 1 point. Soft constraints, like class interval, are not considered i=
n this version. Recent studies like &nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/do=
i/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0015" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=
=3D"Bib0015" id=3D"core-Bib0015-7">15</a>] emphasise what is needed is a ti=
metable that meets all the hard constraints at first. Furthermore, the gene=
tic algorithm applies an early ending strategy. After evaluating the fitnes=
s of all chromosomes in a population, the algorithm immediately stops and r=
eturns the current produced feasible solution if a solution with zero hard =
constraint violations is found [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1=
145/3793811.3793816#Bib0014" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0014=
" id=3D"core-Bib0014-4">14</a>], so it can save computational resources.</d=
iv><div role=3D"paragraph"><i>Selection.</i> We use roulette wheel selectio=
n as our parent selection strategy. This method assumes that the probabilit=
y of selecting an item is directly related to its fitness value. The princi=
ple is similar to a physical roulette wheel. Each item in the population oc=
cupies a sector on the wheel, the better fitness will occupy the larger sec=
tor so that it can be selected with a higher chance &nbsp;[<a href=3D"https=
://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0010" role=3D"doc-biblior=
ef" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0010" id=3D"core-Bib0010-3">10</a>]. In this paper, =
we define the smaller fitness value as indicating a higher quality solution=
 with fewer violations. So we need to transform the smaller fitness value t=
o occupy a bigger sector in the wheel to ensure quality. Specifically, we u=
se the transformation shown in Equation (<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/=
full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#eq1">1</a>) [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi=
