2-3"><h4>4.2.3 Hybrid GA+SA.</h4><div role=3D"paragraph"><i>Hybridisation s=
trategy.</i> The hybrid method follows a memetic (GA with local improvement=
) design in which GA performs global exploration and SA provides local inte=
nsification. Specifically, each GA generation produces offspring via select=
ion, crossover, and mutation. SA is then applied as a local search operator=
 to improve a subset of individuals before they are inserted into the next =
population. This coupling aims to combine GA=E2=80=99s population diversity=
 and global search capability with SA=E2=80=99s ability to refine solutions=
 and escape local minima via controlled uphill acceptance.</div><div role=
=3D"paragraph">After offspring creation, SA is invoked for either (i) all o=
ffspring, or (ii) a fixed elite subset (e.g., the best <i>k</i> offspring b=
y <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>)). In this study, SA refinement is applied to the selec=
ted subset to control computational cost while focusing local search on the=
 most promising candidates. The embedded SA uses the same neighbourhood and=
 acceptance rule as the standalone SA (Equations&nbsp;()=E2=80=93()), but r=
uns for a shorter budget (e.g., a limited number of iterations or until no =
improvement is observed for a given patience window). The refined individua=
l replaces its pre-SA version, and the improved population is then evaluate=
d for feasibility. As with the standalone GA, the hybrid terminates early i=
f any individual reaches <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) =3D 0.</div><div role=3D"paragr=
aph">This design increases the probability of rapidly reaching feasibility =
on highly constrained instances: GA maintains exploration through recombina=
tion and mutation, while SA reduces violations through targeted local moves=
 and probabilistic escape behaviour [<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full=
/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0011" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bi=
b0011" id=3D"core-Bib0011-3">11</a>, <a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full=
/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0015" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bi=
b0015" id=3D"core-Bib0015-11">15</a>].</div></section></section></section><=
