x Iteration:</b> The maximum iteration value defines the iteration number t=
o decrease from the initial temperature to a T(min) value or less than it.<=
/div></div></div></div></section><section id=3D"sec-5-1-4"><h4>5.1.4 Hybrid=
 GA+SA.</h4><div role=3D"paragraph">The hybrid GA+SA framework is designed =
to combine the global search ability of the GA with the local optimisation =
capability of SA. In this design, GA acts as the primary optimiser to explo=
re the overall solution space and identify a near-feasible timetable, while=
 SA acts as the secondary local search method that refines the result when =
necessary. During the execution, GA iteratively evolves the population thro=
ugh selection, crossover, and mutation. If a feasible solution satisfying a=
ll hard constraints (fitness&nbsp;=3D&nbsp;0) is obtained, the algorithm te=
rminates and returns the result immediately. Otherwise, when the hybrid mod=
e is enabled, the near-feasible chromosome generated by GA is passed to SA =
as the initial solution. SA then performs neighbourhood adjustments by rand=
omly selecting a course and reassigning it to another valid (timeslot, room=
) pair according to the dataset=E2=80=99s availability map. The same fitnes=
s evaluation function is reused to ensure consistency. The SA process conti=
nues with a temperature-controlled acceptance rule and a geometric cooling =
schedule until either the minimum temperature threshold or a feasible solut=
ion is reached. This conditional integration reduces unnecessary computatio=
n while still allowing further refinement when GA alone cannot find an opti=
mal timetable. The design effectively balances efficiency and accuracy, dem=
onstrating stable convergence across medium to large-scale benchmark instan=
ces.</div></section><section id=3D"sec-5-1-5"><h4>5.1.5 Stop Condition.</h4=
><div role=3D"paragraph">The GA and SA in this paper are set to stop once a=
 feasible solution with zero hard-constraint violations is identified, and =
no further optimisation of soft constraints is performed. As highlighted by=
 &nbsp;[<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#Bib0=
010" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-xml-rid=3D"Bib0010" id=3D"core-Bib0010-4">=
10</a>], satisfying all hard constraints is regarded as the primary objecti=
ve in university course timetabling, while optimisation of soft constraints=
 is treated as a secondary goal. Therefore, our approach in this paper focu=
s on the primary objective and evaluates efficiency.</div></section><sectio=
n id=3D"sec-5-1-6"><h4>5.1.6 Format.</h4><div role=3D"paragraph">We operate=
d experiments on benchmark instances to validate the proposed model from co=
mp01.ctt (c01) to comp21.ctt (c21)=E2=80=94each belongs to different levels=
 of constraint difficulty: Early, Hidden, and Late. Each dataset defines co=
urses, rooms, days, periods per day, and various hard constraints such as u=
navailability and curriculum conflicts. The experiments aim to assess the a=
lgorithm=E2=80=99s ability to generate timetables under real-world-like con=
straints. Table&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811=
