alability Analysis.</h4><div role=3D"paragraph">To further examine the scal=
ability, we extended one ITC-2007 instance (comp02.ctt) as a larger-sized b=
enchmark. For example, the original comp02 instance contains 82 courses, 16=
 rooms, 56 teachers and 513 constraints. In one of the extended datasets, t=
he number of courses, rooms, and constraints was doubled, resulting in 164 =
courses, 32 rooms, 112 teachers, and 1026 constraints.</div><div role=3D"pa=
ragraph">This doubling of instance size leads to an exponential increase in=
 the search space (from the order of 10<sup>200</sup> to over 10<sup>400</s=
up> possible solutions). Despite this exponential growth, the runtime incre=
ased by approximately three in the extended comp02 dataset, demonstrating t=
hat the hybrid GA+SA approach remains effective and scalable under larger t=
imetabling sizes.</div><div role=3D"paragraph">To ensure consistency, we co=
mpared the GA and SA parameter settings as in Table &nbsp;<a href=3D"https:=
//dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#tab4">4</a> and &nbsp;<a href=
=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#tab5">5</a>. After =
2-10 times courses, rooms, and constraints data than original data, the sea=
rch space expanded exponentially. As a result, the runtime of both SA and h=
ybrid increased by approximately linear times compared to the original ITC-=
2007 benchmarks. This demonstrates that the algorithms remain effective und=
er quickly larger scales. The following graph shows the runtime curve. Fig&=
nbsp;<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#fig2">2=
</a> shows the runtime (milliseconds) trend.</div><div role=3D"paragraph">A=
s shown in Fig&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.=
3793816#fig2">2</a>, standalone GA can find a feasible solution (fitness va=
lue=3D0) for small- to medium-scale instances (c02 - c02*5). However, as th=
e scale continues to increase, both fitness value and runtime have risen, w=
hich indicates a slowing convergence rate. Details of fitness value(violate=
d hard constraints) and runtime can be found in Table&nbsp;<a href=3D"https=
://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3793811.3793816#tab8">8</a>.</div><div role=
=3D"paragraph">For large-scale problems, the standalone GA remains a local =
optimum situation and depends on parameter design. Standalone SA also requi=
res a longer computation time, while the hybrid algorithm demonstrates fast=
er convergence as the dataset expands. This phenomenon demonstrates the com=
bined advantages of GA and SA and excludes their disadvantages.</div><div c=
